# Page & Component Modularity

Pages and route files must stay thin. Do not define constants, helper functions, or UI components inline in a `page.tsx`.

## Structure

| Concern | Location |
|---------|----------|
| Page route | `app/**/page.tsx` — data fetch + render one top-level component |
| Constants | `constants/` or `lib/` |
| Pure helpers | `lib/` |
| Reusable UI | `components/` |
| Section UI | `components/<feature>/` sub-components |

## Page pattern

```tsx
// ✅ GOOD — page only orchestrates
export default async function AdminPage() {
  const storeId = getStaffStoreId();
  if (!storeId) return <StaffSetupNotice title="Admin" message={STAFF_SETUP_MESSAGES.missingStoreId} />;

  const data = await loadAdminDashboardData(storeId);
  return <AdminDashboard {...data} />;
}

// ❌ BAD — inline component, constants, and helpers in page.tsx
const SETUP_TEXT = "...";
function SetupNotice() { ... }
function formatX() { ... }
```

## Component pattern

Break large components into focused pieces. Move derived data builders to `lib/`, visual constants to `constants/`, and repeated UI blocks to child components.

```tsx
// ✅ GOOD
const metrics = buildHeaderMetrics(stats, metrics);
return metrics.map((m) => <HeaderMetricCard key={m.label} {...m} />);

// ❌ BAD — constants, builder logic, and markup all in one file
const COLORS = { ... };
const items = [{ label: "...", value: format(...) }];
return <div>{items.map(...)}</div>;
```
